## NaN-key decision

Every NaN now shares one key, `[:py.numeric/nan]`; this is recorded in §8.5.4.
- A float here is a plain value with no object identity, so one NaN can't be told apart from another.
- Jing already stores every NaN as one canonical value, so a per-object key couldn't survive addressing.
- The old key (the host double) wasn't deterministic: on the JVM, comparing a NaN with itself returns true for the same boxed object and false for two boxes.

This differs from CPython, where two different NaN objects are two keys, but `x = nan; d[x] = 1; d[x]` still works. It needs Architect confirmation.

